[%- macro lane_paragraph(tail) -%]
[%- if lane_protocol -%]
**Lane (QS-332).** This task's lane is `[[ lane ]]`; its phase protocol is rendered below under "Lane protocol" — do not re-read the lane file.
[%- else -%]
**Lane (QS-332).** Also capture `lane` from the context JSON, then read
`docs/workflow/lanes/<lane>.md` — that file is this task's phase
protocol. If `lane` is empty (a pre-existing worktree / legacy
in-flight task — every new task is labelled at birth), fall back to
[docs/workflow/phase-protocols.md](../../docs/workflow/phase-protocols.md)
and surface the backfill guidance: the issue still needs its axis
labels (`gh issue edit <N> --add-label ...`). [[ "This phase is read-only\nwith respect to the gate, so it may proceed on the fallback." if tail == "readonly" else "In this implement\nphase the labels must be resolved **before the first commit** — the\nfirst `--impacted` run fails otherwise (the gate is the backstop, not\na parallel path)." ]]
[%- endif -%]
[%- endmacro -%]
{#- QS-372: the implement-variant rule, owned here once (create-plan's
    "Determine NEXT_PHASE", review-task step 6). `var` is `NEXT_PHASE` or
    `{{next_implement}}`, always assigned the bare phase name. The STOP
    message's prefix is stable so the autonomous build loop (#369) can
    detect it. -#}
[%- macro implement_routing(var) -%]
Route by the **declared target** (QS-321 — never inferred from the
files you touch): re-run `python scripts/qs/context.py`. If `context.py`
exits non-zero / prints no JSON, or returns empty `labels` **and**
`title`, the `gh` lookup failed: **STOP** and print:

```text
Cannot pick the implement variant: the issue lookup failed (gh) — retry, or tell me which variant to run.
```

Do **not** print the label message or commands below. Otherwise take
`target`. `factory` → `[[ var ]] = implement-setup-task`; `product` →
`[[ var ]] = implement-task`. For any other value (empty — no or
ambiguous `target:*` label), **STOP** and print (substitute `{{issue}}`
with the issue number from `context.py` in the messages and commands
below):

```text
Cannot pick the implement variant: issue #{{issue}} has no single target:* label. Label it with exactly one of target:factory or target:product (see the commands below), or tell me which variant to run.
```

Print the commands below to the user as well. **Do NOT run any of them
yourself** — the target label is the user's declaration (QS-321). The
user runs exactly one:

```bash
gh issue edit {{issue}} --add-label target:factory
```

```bash
gh issue edit {{issue}} --add-label target:product
```

If `labels` already carries **both** target labels, tell the user to
remove the wrong one — `gh issue edit {{issue}} --remove-label target:factory`
or `gh issue edit {{issue}} --remove-label target:product` (the one that
is wrong; concrete labels only).

After printing, wait for the user: if they report adding a label, re-run
`context.py` and route again; if they name `implement-task` /
`implement-setup-task` (with or without a leading `/`), or `factory` /
`product` (map `factory` → `implement-setup-task`, `product` →
`implement-task`), use it; any other answer → ask again.
[%- endmacro -%]
{#- QS-372: the phase handoff, written once for both harnesses. Call it as
    `[% call(prefix) m.handoff("<next_cmd>") %]` with banner lines
    (`[[ prefix ]] ...`) as the body. On Claude the user-facing block is the
    launcher's `handoff_text` (scripts/qs/launchers/claude.py), printed
    verbatim; on OpenCode the agent itself runs `new_context`, so its
    contract stays prose. `fix_plan` adds the review-fix flags and the
    existing-session block; `rerun` is the phase re-run after the fix. -#}
[% macro handoff(next_cmd, fix_plan=false, rerun="") %]
[% set banner = caller("✅" if harness == "claude" else "[OK]") | trim %]
**Before running** — substitute `{{worktree}}`, `{{issue}}`, `{{title}}`[[ ",\n`{{fix_plan_path}}`, `{{pr_number}}`" if fix_plan else "" ]]
with the values you captured earlier[[ (";\nsubstitute `" ~ next_cmd ~ "` with the value you resolved above") if next_cmd.startswith("{{") else "" ]].

```bash
python scripts/qs/next_step.py \
    --next-cmd "[[ next_cmd ]]" \
    --work-dir "{{worktree}}" \
    --issue {{issue}} \
    --title "{{title}}" \
[% if fix_plan %]
    --fix-plan-path "{{fix_plan_path}}" \
    --pr-number {{pr_number}} \
[% endif %]
    --harness [[ "claude-code" if harness == "claude" else "opencode" ]]
```

[% if harness == "claude" %]
Parse the JSON. If the command exits non-zero or prints no JSON, if the
JSON contains an `error` key, or if it has no `handoff_text`, STOP and
print the raw output verbatim. Otherwise print the report below,
replacing `<handoff_text>` with the payload's `handoff_text` **verbatim**
(do not rephrase, reorder or drop any part of it):

```text
[% if banner %]
[[ banner ]]

[% endif %]
<handoff_text>
[% if rerun %]

Then re-run /[[ rerun ]] (or open a fresh `claude --agent qs-[[ rerun ]]`
session) to verify.
[% endif %]
```
[% else %]
Parse the JSON output of ``next_step.py``.

**If `next_step.py` exits non-zero or prints no JSON, or its JSON has no
`new_context`**, STOP and print the raw output verbatim.

**If the `next_step.py` JSON contains an `error` key**, STOP and print
the raw JSON to the user. Do not proceed to run `new_context`.

[% if fix_plan %]
Otherwise capture the ``new_context`` string and the
``existing_session_prompt`` value. The ``existing_session_prompt`` is
a paste-into-already-running-session prompt — it is NOT a
session-spawn command. Do NOT execute it. **If
`existing_session_prompt` is missing or null, omit the "Already
running an implementation session?" block from the user report;
only emit it when the field is a non-empty string**.
[% else %]
Otherwise capture the ``new_context`` string.
[% endif %]

**Run `new_context` via the Bash tool**. The string is a
``python scripts/qs/spawn_session.py --agent qs-<phase> --directory
<wd> --title ... --prompt ...`` invocation — already inside the
allow-listed ``python scripts/qs/*`` pattern. Do NOT extract only the
prompt and send it to the current session. Do NOT strip
``--agent qs-<phase>``. The ``--agent`` flag is what binds the
next-phase orchestrator to the new session via OpenCode's HTTP API
``POST /session/<id>/prompt_async`` body — strip it and the prompt
lands on the default agent, breaking the pipeline silently.

**If the Bash tool returns an error before producing any JSON output**
(e.g., permission denied, missing interpreter), STOP and print the
Bash tool's error message verbatim. Do not attempt to parse JSON.

Parse the stdout of that command as JSON. The success contract is
**binary**:

- ``status == "session_created"`` AND ``agent`` equals `qs-` followed
  by the phase name passed to `--next-cmd` (here: `qs-[[ next_cmd ]]`)
  → success; report to the user:

  ```text
[% if banner %]
[[ banner | indent(2, first=true) ]]
[% endif %]
  [OK] Next phase session created: qs-[[ next_cmd ]]
       (visible in the OpenCode session list on the left)
[% if fix_plan %]

  Already running an implementation session?
  Paste this prompt into it:
    {{existing_session_prompt}}
[% endif %]
[% if rerun %]

  Then re-activate `qs-[[ rerun ]]` (or open a fresh session bound to
  it) to verify.
[% endif %]
  ```
[% if fix_plan %]

  (Omit the "Already running an implementation session?" block when
  `existing_session_prompt` is missing or null.)
[% endif %]

- **Anything else** (any other ``status`` value, missing or mismatched
  ``agent`` field, non-zero exit code, malformed JSON) → STOP. Print
  the raw JSON output verbatim to the user. Do NOT claim the next
  phase started. The user inspects the JSON and acts on the specific
  failure mode (``agent_file_missing``, ``agent_file_unreadable``,
  ``agent_file_empty``, ``worktree_invalid``, ``fallback_cli``,
  ``fallback_unavailable``, ``session_orphaned`` — each documented in
  ``scripts/qs/spawn_session.py``).
[% endif %]
[% endmacro %]
